<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isis was a major goddess of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, revered as a protector of kingship, a healer and a model of devoted motherhood. Her cult grew in the Late and Ptolemaic periods, with great temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behbeit-el-hagar">Behbeit el-Hagar</a>, and spread across the Greek and Roman world. Her temple at Philae was among the last Egyptian temples in use, until its closure under Justinian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isis.jpg" alt="Isis" width="320" height="703" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isis.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pagan institutions faded meanwhile: the Serapeum of Alexandria was destroyed in 391, the philosopher Hypatia was murdered by a Christian mob in 415, and Justinian had the temple of <b>Isis</b> at Philae closed in the 530s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From around the 24th century BCE the god Osiris, a king murdered by his brother Seth and restored to life by his sister and wife <b>Isis</b>, rose to great importance as lord of the dead.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Hellenistic kings were worshipped as gods, and new cults spread, such as those of Serapis, <b>Isis</b> and Fortune.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Churches such as Santa Prisca and San Clemente were built over mithraea, and Mithras, unlike <b>Isis</b>, was forgotten in late antiquity.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Vergil’s Aeneid gave the Imperial vision of the past, Pliny the Elder wrote a sceptical history of magic, and Apuleius’s The Golden Ass almost certainly describes the mysteries of <b>Isis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ptolemies of Egypt promoted Serapis, a god combining Egyptian and Greek features, and the cult of <b>Isis</b> spread from Delos to Pompeii, Rome and the Rhine frontier.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li></ul></section>
